-MAN: This is the time

260
00:09:34,842 --> 00:09:36,010
for pleasant discussion

261
00:09:36,043 --> 00:09:38,612
in a thoroughly relaxed mood.

262
00:09:38,645 --> 00:09:40,447
They don't pick this time
of the day

263
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:43,583
to spring unpleasant surprises
on dad.

264
00:09:43,617 --> 00:09:44,985
If they have disagreeable news,

265
00:09:45,018 --> 00:09:46,320
they will postpone
the discussion

266
00:09:46,353 --> 00:09:48,522
- until another time.
-CROW: When he's asleep.

267
00:09:48,555 --> 00:09:50,290
MAN:
This is no time to dun father

268
00:09:50,323 --> 00:09:52,225
for a raise in your allowance,

269
00:09:52,225 --> 00:09:53,760
new clothes, or argue

270
00:09:53,827 --> 00:09:55,963
about other financial matters.

271
00:09:56,963 --> 00:09:58,532
MIKE:
Father, I had a feeling today.

272
00:09:58,565 --> 00:10:00,467
-SERVO: Well, don't, son.
-MIKE: Okay.

273
00:10:00,533 --> 00:10:02,670
MAN: Ah, dinner time.

274
00:10:02,737 --> 00:10:05,572
Yes, mother, Junior
remembered to clean up.

275
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:07,342
CROW:
You emasculating bint.

276
00:10:07,342 --> 00:10:10,645
MIKE: Hi, I'm Betty, and I
will be your wife tonight.

277
00:10:10,645 --> 00:10:12,780
SERVO: Well,
this certainly is pleasant.

278
00:10:14,415 --> 00:10:16,150
MAN: Brother seats Junior.

279
00:10:16,183 --> 00:10:18,252
CROW: Daughter
obsesses with the flowers.

280
00:10:18,285 --> 00:10:19,620
MIKE: [ Chuckles ]

281
00:10:19,653 --> 00:10:21,755
MAN: Then helps mother
to her chair

282
00:10:21,788 --> 00:10:23,590
as he would his best girl.

283
00:10:23,623 --> 00:10:25,625
CROW: The less said
about this, the better.

284
00:10:25,660 --> 00:10:27,462
SERVO: Yeah. I got it. I got it.

285
00:10:27,495 --> 00:10:28,795
I got it, honey.

286
00:10:28,828 --> 00:10:31,398
CROW: Junior seats dad
and sister seats the dog,

287
00:10:31,432 --> 00:10:33,000
-and the dog...
-MAN: Well, the dinner date

288
00:10:33,033 --> 00:10:35,635
has begun,
and they're all happy about it.

289
00:10:35,670 --> 00:10:37,972
MIKE: Their stomachs
knotted like fists.

290
00:10:38,005 --> 00:10:40,473
SERVO: Dad, I'm dating a Negro.

291
00:10:40,507 --> 00:10:41,675
MIKE:
Anyone seen our waitress?

292
00:10:41,708 --> 00:10:43,477
MAN: Many families
throughout the country

293
00:10:43,510 --> 00:10:46,713
observe the custom
of saying grace at meal time.

294
00:10:46,747 --> 00:10:48,082
CROW:
Please, God. Take me now.

295
00:10:48,115 --> 00:10:51,852
MAN: It is always treated
with reverence and respect.

296
00:10:51,885 --> 00:10:53,853
SERVO: Field Marshal Montgomery
sits down to eat.

297
00:10:53,887 --> 00:10:56,457
Yes, it's smashing,
it's a good day.

298
00:10:56,490 --> 00:10:59,827
MAN: Napkins on the lap,
the family awaits service.

299
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:02,028
They converse pleasantly
while dad serves.

300
00:11:02,062 --> 00:11:03,663
MIKE: No, I'll just
have a saltine...

301
00:11:03,697 --> 00:11:04,965
MAN: I said pleasantly

302
00:11:04,998 --> 00:11:07,768
for that is the keynote
at dinnertime.

303
00:11:07,802 --> 00:11:10,772
It is not only good manners
but good sense.

304
00:11:10,805 --> 00:11:13,007
CROW:
Emotions are for ethnic people.

305
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:16,277
MAN: Pleasant, un-emotional
conversation helps digestion.

306
00:11:16,310 --> 00:11:18,378
SERVO: I can't stress
unemotional enough.

307
00:11:18,412 --> 00:11:21,682
MAN: Father serves mother first,
then daughter.

308
00:11:21,715 --> 00:11:23,350
MIKE: Let's go
to the flowchart for this.

309
00:11:23,383 --> 00:11:24,652
MAN: Then the boys.

310
00:11:24,685 --> 00:11:26,253
Don't worry, fellas,
you won't get left.

311
00:11:26,287 --> 00:11:29,057
CROW: And be sure to make
a plate for the narrator.

312
00:11:29,090 --> 00:11:30,690
SERVO: A violent argument erupts

313
00:11:30,725 --> 00:11:32,927
over whose day
was more pleasant.

314
00:11:35,028 --> 00:11:36,197
CROW:
Can I have chopsticks, please?

315
00:11:36,230 --> 00:11:38,432
MAN:
Help to cut your meat, Junior?

316
00:11:38,465 --> 00:11:40,100
Of course.

317
00:11:40,133 --> 00:11:41,068
SERVO: Ow, ow, ow,
that's my hand.

318
00:11:41,102 --> 00:11:42,403
-Ow.
-MAN: By example,

319
00:11:42,437 --> 00:11:44,472
older children
can teach younger ones

320
00:11:44,505 --> 00:11:45,605
good table manners.

321
00:11:45,638 --> 00:11:46,907
SERVO:
Remember, always cut the meat

322
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:49,210
of the person to your left.

323
00:11:49,243 --> 00:11:51,345
CROW: You don't have
to cut my mashed potatoes, too,

324
00:11:51,378 --> 00:11:52,480
not the Jell-O.

325
00:11:52,513 --> 00:11:53,613
MAN: And no one starts eating

326
00:11:53,647 --> 00:11:55,382
until father has
served himself.

327
00:11:55,415 --> 00:11:57,217
-CROW: This means you.
-MAN: Always wait

328
00:11:57,250 --> 00:11:59,253
for the hostess,
in this case, mother,

329
00:11:59,287 --> 00:12:01,722
to begin eating
before you start.

330
00:12:01,755 --> 00:12:03,223
MIKE: Father feigns eating,
draws Junior out,

331
00:12:03,257 --> 00:12:04,858
-then disowns him.
-MAN: Let father and mother

332
00:12:04,892 --> 00:12:07,895
guide the conversational
trend if they desire.

333
00:12:07,928 --> 00:12:11,032
After all, they made
all this possible,

334
00:12:11,065 --> 00:12:13,967
and they want to talk
over their day with each other.

335
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:15,735
CROW:
I'm moving to Fire Island, dear.

336
00:12:15,770 --> 00:12:17,538
MAN: Tell mother
how good the food is.

337
00:12:17,572 --> 00:12:18,805
MIKE: Even as you gag on it.

338
00:12:18,838 --> 00:12:20,073
MAN: ... a compliment, too.

339
00:12:20,107 --> 00:12:21,708
-MIKE: This stinks.
-MAN: It makes them want

340
00:12:21,742 --> 00:12:23,043
to continue pleasing you.

341
00:12:23,077 --> 00:12:24,645
CROW: After all,
that's why God put women

342
00:12:24,678 --> 00:12:25,913
on this green Earth.

343
00:12:25,947 --> 00:12:28,048
MAN: Well, all of this
is good general advice,

344
00:12:28,082 --> 00:12:30,383
but there are
some specific don'ts.

345
00:12:30,417 --> 00:12:33,853
Maybe the family
will demonstrate for us.

346
00:12:33,887 --> 00:12:35,957
Don't monopolize
the conversation

347
00:12:35,990 --> 00:12:38,792
and go on and on
without stopping.

348
00:12:38,825 --> 00:12:39,893
-MIKE: God.
-MAN: Nothing destroys

349
00:12:39,927 --> 00:12:41,762
the charm of a meal
more quickly.

350
00:12:41,795 --> 00:12:43,963
MIKE: Than having a personality.

351
00:12:43,997 --> 00:12:46,600
MAN: To say that the rest
of the group is bored

352
00:12:46,633 --> 00:12:48,602
would be a gross
understatement.

353
00:12:48,635 --> 00:12:50,838
SERVO: Mother, do something.

354
00:12:50,872 --> 00:12:53,407
CROW: At least I have two sons.

355
00:12:55,742 --> 00:12:58,212
MAN:
Don't discuss unpleasant topics

356
00:12:58,245 --> 00:13:00,915
such as gruesome sights
or sounds...

357
00:13:00,948 --> 00:13:02,683
SERVO: So then sister
made Patrick get him up...

358
00:13:02,717 --> 00:13:04,652
MAN:
...or unpleasant occurrences.

359
00:13:04,685 --> 00:13:06,220
This is a time of pleasure,

360
00:13:06,253 --> 00:13:09,423
of charm, and relaxation,
remember?

361
00:13:09,457 --> 00:13:11,992
MIKE: I have no son.

362
00:13:14,795 --> 00:13:16,663
MAN:
Don't make unkind comparisons

363
00:13:16,697 --> 00:13:18,432
about your stand of living.

364
00:13:18,465 --> 00:13:21,835
The dinner table is no place
for discontent.

365
00:13:21,868 --> 00:13:23,770
It makes dad and mother

366
00:13:23,803 --> 00:13:26,307
uncomfortable and unhappy.

367
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:28,308
CROW: And they already
dislike you enough.

368
00:13:28,342 --> 00:13:29,410
SERVO: Mm-hmm.

369
00:13:29,443 --> 00:13:32,980
Well, there it is, spankings
all around, then.

370
00:13:33,013 --> 00:13:35,315
Oh, and she was a big nun
with a mustache,

371
00:13:35,348 --> 00:13:37,852
-this huge black...
-MAN: Don't insult your brother

372
00:13:37,885 --> 00:13:41,422
or sister with offensive
remarks about friends.

373
00:13:41,455 --> 00:13:43,390
Arguments, harsh words,

374
00:13:43,423 --> 00:13:46,060
mean tension,
and distress for everyone.

375
00:13:46,093 --> 00:13:48,228
SERVO: Yeah, go piss up a rope.

376
00:13:48,262 --> 00:13:50,932
CROW:
Sit and spin you cruddy skank.

377
00:13:52,132 --> 00:13:55,035
SERVO: Perhaps booze
would alleviate this situation.

378
00:13:56,570 --> 00:13:58,472
MAN: Do remember simply this,

379
00:13:58,505 --> 00:14:00,740
treat the other members
of your family

380
00:14:00,773 --> 00:14:03,343
with the same respect
and cordiality

381
00:14:03,377 --> 00:14:05,112
you would show your most
treasured friend

382
00:14:05,145 --> 00:14:06,547
outside the family circle.

383
00:14:06,580 --> 00:14:08,882
-SERVO: So snap dad's bra?
-MAN: This does not mean

384
00:14:08,915 --> 00:14:10,417
you should be stiff or formal.

385
00:14:10,450 --> 00:14:13,353
With your own family,
you can relax.

386
00:14:13,387 --> 00:14:15,055
Be yourself.

387
00:14:15,088 --> 00:14:17,290
Just be sure
it's your best self.

388
00:14:17,323 --> 00:14:18,958
CROW: And be sure
no one knows the real you.

389
00:14:18,992 --> 00:14:20,127
MAN: This is better.

390
00:14:20,160 --> 00:14:21,462
SERVO: Father
does his ferret impression.

391
00:14:21,495 --> 00:14:23,830
MAN: Now all things
are on an even keel again.

392
00:14:23,863 --> 00:14:26,100
MIKE: And everyone wants to flee
the seething cauldron of angst.

393
00:14:26,133 --> 00:14:28,302
MAN:
Now we're ready for dessert.

394
00:14:28,335 --> 00:14:30,203
Most families don't have maids.

395
00:14:30,237 --> 00:14:32,038
-CROW: They have daughters.
-MAN: So mother needs help

396
00:14:32,072 --> 00:14:34,842
with the serving
from the kitchen.

397
00:14:34,875 --> 00:14:36,710
Son says mother
has done enough.

398
00:14:36,743 --> 00:14:38,112
Watch him play butler.

399
00:14:38,145 --> 00:14:41,415
SERVO: In "Remains of the Day."

400
00:14:41,448 --> 00:14:43,850
MAN: The removal of dishes
from the main course

401
00:14:43,883 --> 00:14:45,718
and the serving of dessert
set the stage

402
00:14:45,752 --> 00:14:48,488
for the conclusion
of a thoroughly pleasant meal.

403
00:14:48,522 --> 00:14:49,957
SERVO: I probably should've
washed my hands

404
00:14:49,990 --> 00:14:52,927
after handling that dead
woodchuck, oh, well.

405
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:53,993
-You want gin, pop?
-No.

406
00:14:54,027 --> 00:14:55,295
MAN: Do you begin to see now

407
00:14:55,328 --> 00:14:56,997
how a date with your family

408
00:14:57,030 --> 00:14:58,765
can be a truly
special occasion?

409
00:14:58,798 --> 00:15:01,302
-SERVO: Do you? Do you?
-MAN: Why brother and sister

410
00:15:01,335 --> 00:15:02,970
looked forward to the evening?

411
00:15:03,003 --> 00:15:05,905
SERVO: Well, do you?
Better say yes, damn it!

412
00:15:05,940 --> 00:15:07,240
MAN:
When the dinner hour at home

413
00:15:07,273 --> 00:15:09,510
is treated with a certain
amount of graciousness

414
00:15:09,543 --> 00:15:12,680
and ceremony,
it can be memorable.

415
00:15:12,713 --> 00:15:14,515
There is not family so poor,

416
00:15:14,548 --> 00:15:16,950
but that the evening meal
can be eaten

417
00:15:16,983 --> 00:15:19,018
in an atmosphere
of warmth and gentleness.

418
00:15:19,052 --> 00:15:20,720
SERVO: And control
and repression.

419
00:15:20,753 --> 00:15:22,188
MAN: There is no family so busy,

420
00:15:22,222 --> 00:15:24,992
but that it can come together
in the evening

421
00:15:25,025 --> 00:15:27,160
for a dinner date,
which will give its members

422
00:15:27,193 --> 00:15:29,997
something to look back upon
with happiness

423
00:15:30,030 --> 00:15:31,298
all their days.

424
00:15:31,332 --> 00:15:33,067
CROW: You know,
this makes me wanna heat up

425
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:35,002
a Libbyland dinner
and eat in front of the TV.

426
00:15:35,035 --> 00:15:36,303
-MIKE: Mm-hmm.
-SERVO: Now, enjoy

427
00:15:36,337 --> 00:15:38,472
some refreshments in the lobby.

428
00:15:38,505 --> 00:15:39,607
MIKE: I'll buy.

429
00:15:39,640 --> 00:15:41,042
CROW: A Simmel-Meservey release.

430
00:15:41,075 --> 00:15:44,277
[ Music playing ]

431
00:15:48,615 --> 00:15:49,917
MIKE: Invasion USA?

432
00:15:49,950 --> 00:15:51,118
CROW: Wow.

433
00:15:51,152 --> 00:15:53,320
MIKE: Starring these people
and Chuck Norris.

434
00:15:53,353 --> 00:15:54,422
CROW: Ooh.

435
00:15:56,590 --> 00:16:00,260
SERVO: And the entire
Gotham City council.

436
00:16:00,293 --> 00:16:01,695
MIKE: Let's see, Phyllis Coates,

437
00:16:01,728 --> 00:16:04,498
hey, there's two Lois Lanes
in this movie, at least.

438
00:16:04,532 --> 00:16:05,565
CROW: Yeah.

439
00:16:07,100 --> 00:16:08,502
SERVO: What do you know?

440
00:16:08,535 --> 00:16:10,970
What? And the lead singer of
The Cure is a screenwriter, too.

441
00:16:11,005 --> 00:16:12,338
I had no idea.

442
00:16:13,940 --> 00:16:17,643
CROW: [ Makes sound ]

443
00:16:17,678 --> 00:16:21,748
With Albert Glasser conducting
the Invasion USA orchestra.

444
00:16:21,782 --> 00:16:23,850
[ Imitates trumpet ]

445
00:16:23,883 --> 00:16:25,085
SERVO: Relentless.

446
00:16:25,118 --> 00:16:27,253
This music is like
a good Danny Elfman tune.

447
00:16:27,287 --> 00:16:28,588
CROW: You mean it doesn't exist?

448
00:16:28,655 --> 00:16:29,657
SERVO: Boom.

449
00:16:31,458 --> 00:16:33,493
MIKE: Hey, you know,
I think I had an apartment

450
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:34,962
-in this building over here.
-SERVO: Yeah?

451
00:16:34,995 --> 00:16:36,163
Oh, did you now?

452
00:16:36,197 --> 00:16:37,663
-Cold water flat...
-CROW: Looks like

453
00:16:37,698 --> 00:16:41,468
James Sullivan really had
his work cut out for him.

454
00:16:41,468 --> 00:16:43,070
-MIKE: Cut out.
-CROW: Oh, stop it.

455
00:16:43,103 --> 00:16:44,672
SERVO: Yeah.

456
00:16:44,705 --> 00:16:46,640
MIKE: Let's get on
with it here, please.

457
00:16:46,673 --> 00:16:49,208
SERVO: Please, yeah.

458
00:16:49,242 --> 00:16:51,078
Ah, the Zugsmith touch,

459
00:16:51,112 --> 00:16:52,578
dry but warm.

460
00:16:52,612 --> 00:16:54,682
[ Music playing ]

461
00:16:54,715 --> 00:16:56,717
CROW: What? Me direct?

462
00:16:59,352 --> 00:17:01,088
MIKE: Ah, bustling city music.

463
00:17:01,122 --> 00:17:02,255
SERVO: Of course.

464
00:17:02,288 --> 00:17:05,258
Thinville,
the thinnest city in the world.

465
00:17:05,292 --> 00:17:06,460
CROW: Why does my back hurt?

466
00:17:06,493 --> 00:17:08,295
Must be lifting
this thing wrong.

467
00:17:08,328 --> 00:17:11,432
MIKE:
Oh, thank heavens, civilization.

468
00:17:11,465 --> 00:17:13,233
SERVO: ♪ Cucaracha,
la cucaracha ♪

469
00:17:13,267 --> 00:17:14,602
-[ Speaking Spanish ]
-Ladies and gentlemen,

470
00:17:14,635 --> 00:17:16,870
I'll have a complete sports
roundup for you

471
00:17:16,903 --> 00:17:18,138
in just a moment.

472
00:17:18,172 --> 00:17:19,807
CROW: That guy's just
sitting in the next room.

473
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:20,807
MAN: Another bourbon.

474
00:17:20,840 --> 00:17:21,875
Coming right up.

475
00:17:21,908 --> 00:17:24,243
MIKE:
Shouldn't he be guarding Oswald?

476
00:17:24,277 --> 00:17:26,080
SERVO:
Ah, breakfast at Harry's bar,

477
00:17:26,113 --> 00:17:27,882
cornflakes with gin.

478
00:17:27,915 --> 00:17:30,250
Here's the news bulletin
from The Nation's Capital.

479
00:17:30,283 --> 00:17:32,618
Washington officially
this morning denies rumors

480
00:17:32,652 --> 00:17:34,988
of enemy planes
over northern Alaska.

481
00:17:35,022 --> 00:17:36,357
Meanwhile,
there has been no lessening

482
00:17:36,390 --> 00:17:38,025
of international tension

483
00:17:38,058 --> 00:17:39,993
and informed sources
refuse to discount

484
00:17:40,027 --> 00:17:42,195
the possibility of all-out war.

485
00:17:42,228 --> 00:17:43,997
MIKE: Yeah, David Byrne.

486
00:17:44,030 --> 00:17:47,033
I got enough war talk at home.

487
00:17:47,067 --> 00:17:48,735
You can say that again.

488
00:17:48,768 --> 00:17:51,105
Meanwhile in Italy,
the communists caused another...

489
00:17:51,138 --> 00:17:52,438
Quiet, Jay.

490
00:17:52,472 --> 00:17:54,742
- ...as communist march on...
-SERVO: That's nice.

491
00:17:54,775 --> 00:17:55,942
That'll hold you.

492
00:17:56,877 --> 00:17:58,945
MIKE: I'm still here barkeep.

493
00:18:00,713 --> 00:18:02,348
I wish I could keep him
that quiet

494
00:18:02,382 --> 00:18:03,850
when he comes in here.

495
00:18:03,883 --> 00:18:05,718
-Customer of yours?
-Yeah.

496
00:18:05,752 --> 00:18:07,988
The TV station's
right down the street.

497
00:18:08,022 --> 00:18:09,255
CROW:
He's a chainsaw sculpture.

498
00:18:09,288 --> 00:18:10,957
And what do you think
the lug drinks?

499
00:18:10,990 --> 00:18:13,027
MAN: A sherry flip.

500
00:18:13,060 --> 00:18:14,662
Oh, you must know him, huh?

501
00:18:14,695 --> 00:18:16,963
MIKE: Excuse me, I'm right
in the middle of Slow Waltz

502
00:18:16,997 --> 00:18:17,998
in Cedar Bend.

503
00:18:18,032 --> 00:18:19,633
-You've seen him in here?
-No.

504
00:18:19,667 --> 00:18:21,100
MAN: Well, then,
how did you know...?

505
00:18:21,135 --> 00:18:22,235
He looks like the sort of person

506
00:18:22,268 --> 00:18:24,337
who'd drink a sherry flip.

507
00:18:24,370 --> 00:18:27,240
Say, he does at that!

508
00:18:27,273 --> 00:18:28,508
SERVO: I don't get it.

509
00:18:31,212 --> 00:18:33,047
-MIKE: Kramer.
-SERVO: Hey.

510
00:18:33,080 --> 00:18:34,915
All right, Tim,
how about a beer?

511
00:18:34,948 --> 00:18:37,217
SERVO: You can
all admire me now.

512
00:18:37,250 --> 00:18:39,152
-MAN: Is you workin' today?
-Yeah, but I thought

513
00:18:39,185 --> 00:18:40,553
I'd work in a nice, cool bar.

514
00:18:40,587 --> 00:18:42,722
SERVO:
And pound drink after drink.

515
00:18:42,755 --> 00:18:43,490
...you, sir?

516
00:18:43,523 --> 00:18:44,625
My name is Potter, Vince Potter.

517
00:18:44,658 --> 00:18:47,260
I'm a commentator for
station TVNY.

518
00:18:47,293 --> 00:18:48,428
Would you mind answering
a question

519
00:18:48,462 --> 00:18:50,663
-for tomorrow's program?
-SERVO: The Beatles.

520
00:18:50,697 --> 00:18:52,198
You're gonna use it
on television?

521
00:18:52,232 --> 00:18:53,567
-CROW: Pilgrim.
-That's the idea.

522
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:56,237
Well, gosh, I'll be glad to.

523
00:18:56,270 --> 00:18:58,838
Good. First, I'll get your name
and address, huh?

524
00:18:58,872 --> 00:19:00,307
Ed Mulberry.

525
00:19:00,340 --> 00:19:02,575
Boulder County, Arizona.

526
00:19:02,608 --> 00:19:03,643
-Arizona?
-SERVO: I came here

527
00:19:03,677 --> 00:19:06,078
-to be a gigolo.
-I'm a cattle raiser.

528
00:19:06,112 --> 00:19:06,947
Cattle raiser?

529
00:19:06,980 --> 00:19:08,348
MIKE: Races cattle, okay.

530
00:19:08,382 --> 00:19:09,517
Now, here's today's question.

531
00:19:09,550 --> 00:19:12,018
Are you for or against
the universal draft?

532
00:19:12,052 --> 00:19:13,587
Now, by that I mean,
draft everything.

533
00:19:13,620 --> 00:19:15,722
Soldiers for the army,
factories for war work,

534
00:19:15,755 --> 00:19:17,557
labor for factories,
the whole business.

535
00:19:17,590 --> 00:19:19,458
-Well...
-CROW: My puzzler hurts.

536
00:19:19,492 --> 00:19:22,295
...government
in business already.

537
00:19:22,328 --> 00:19:23,697
-You take cattle now.
-SERVO: Mm-hmm.

538
00:19:23,730 --> 00:19:26,165
One bureau shoves the cost
of feed up

539
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:28,968
and another keeps the price
of beef down.

540
00:19:29,002 --> 00:19:31,372
That's why I cut my herd
down to 3,000 head this year.

541
00:19:31,405 --> 00:19:32,805
SERVO: It's great,
but it's not the question.

542
00:19:32,838 --> 00:19:34,675
I would raise 5,000
if they hadn't put

543
00:19:34,708 --> 00:19:36,443
price controls on.

544
00:19:36,477 --> 00:19:38,345
-Another bourbon.
-CROW: Make it a quadruple.

545
00:19:38,378 --> 00:19:42,015
And they soak you with those
income taxes, oh, brother.

546
00:19:42,048 --> 00:19:43,483
CROW: Scanners.

547
00:19:44,552 --> 00:19:46,987
You pay a high income tax?

548
00:19:47,020 --> 00:19:48,055
Plenty.

549
00:19:48,088 --> 00:19:49,823
You must make
a great deal of money.

550
00:19:49,857 --> 00:19:51,458
I do all right.

551
00:19:51,492 --> 00:19:53,693
MIKE: Make more than you,
you fruit.

552
00:19:53,727 --> 00:19:54,895
Down with taxes.

553
00:19:56,195 --> 00:19:59,332
SERVO: Pass the beer nuts,
you back ass cracker.

554
00:19:59,365 --> 00:20:02,835
MIKE: The conversation
takes an abrupt shift.

555
00:20:02,868 --> 00:20:05,172
SERVO: I don't stand
a chance, do I?

556
00:20:05,205 --> 00:20:06,473
What would you like, honey?

557
00:20:06,507 --> 00:20:07,673
A scotch over ice.

558
00:20:07,707 --> 00:20:10,310
I don't know why you
New Yorkers drink scotch.

559
00:20:10,343 --> 00:20:11,578
Stuff tastes
like medicine to me.

560
00:20:11,612 --> 00:20:13,913
What do you drink
in San Francisco?

561
00:20:13,947 --> 00:20:15,115
-Daiquiri.
-MIKE: Hmm, nice.

562
00:20:15,148 --> 00:20:17,150
That sounds
like a sissy drink to me.

563
00:20:17,183 --> 00:20:19,485
I just like the cracked ice.

564
00:20:19,518 --> 00:20:22,088
Scotch on a cracked ice.

565
00:20:22,122 --> 00:20:23,557
CROW: [ Makes sound ]

566
00:20:23,590 --> 00:20:25,025
VINCE: I beg your pardon.

567
00:20:25,058 --> 00:20:26,960
-I'm Vince Potter.
-Yeah?

568
00:20:26,993 --> 00:20:29,195
At television station TVNY.

569
00:20:29,228 --> 00:20:30,363
Oh, yes.

570
00:20:30,397 --> 00:20:32,132
I'd like to ask you
both a question.

571
00:20:32,165 --> 00:20:34,233
But, first, may I have
your name, sir?

572
00:20:34,267 --> 00:20:36,837
Sylvester. George Sylvester.

573
00:20:36,870 --> 00:20:38,272
Your occupation?

574
00:20:38,305 --> 00:20:40,007
I'm a tractor manufacturer.

575
00:20:40,040 --> 00:20:42,342
CROW: A daiquiri swilling
tractor manufacturer, okay.

576
00:20:42,375 --> 00:20:43,610
-Your name.
-SERVO: The worst.

577
00:20:43,643 --> 00:20:45,712
CROW: And your breasts?
Uh, I mean occupation?

578
00:20:45,745 --> 00:20:48,315
-[ Indistinct ]
-Telephone number?

579
00:20:48,348 --> 00:20:49,883
1717...

580
00:20:49,917 --> 00:20:51,852
-Now, look here...
-It's all right, George.

581
00:20:51,885 --> 00:20:53,753
I listen to Mr. Potter
every Friday.

582
00:20:53,787 --> 00:20:55,888
His program is very popular
in New York.

583
00:20:55,922 --> 00:20:56,923
So is he, evidently.

584
00:20:56,957 --> 00:20:58,158
Thank you.

585
00:20:58,192 --> 00:21:00,227
Now, here's tonight's question,
Mr. Sylvester.

586
00:21:00,260 --> 00:21:02,795
Are you for or against
the universal draft,

587
00:21:02,828 --> 00:21:04,632
army, labor,
factory, everything?

588
00:21:04,665 --> 00:21:06,567
SERVO: Oh, lights out,
everybody drink up.

589
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:09,002
-...communism?
-Communism?

590
00:21:09,035 --> 00:21:09,770
That's a good quote.

591
00:21:09,803 --> 00:21:11,372
They tried that
on me last week.

592
00:21:11,405 --> 00:21:12,638
But I told 'em where to get off.

593
00:21:12,672 --> 00:21:13,773
Last week? Where was that?

594
00:21:13,807 --> 00:21:15,208
-My plant.
-SERVO: In Moscow.

595
00:21:15,242 --> 00:21:16,943
They sent an army major in
to see me.

596
00:21:16,977 --> 00:21:18,845
It would take me six months
to get in production

597
00:21:18,878 --> 00:21:20,247
on these tank parts.

598
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,615
In the meantime, what would
happen to my tractors, sir?

599
00:21:22,648 --> 00:21:25,218
The army needs tanks,
Mr. Sylvester.

600
00:21:25,252 --> 00:21:26,520
Oh, sure.
I'd like to hear you tell that

601
00:21:26,553 --> 00:21:27,587
to some of my dealers.

602
00:21:27,620 --> 00:21:29,022
If your dealers
are good Americans,

603
00:21:29,055 --> 00:21:31,492
they'll know that tanks are
more important than tractors.

604
00:21:31,525 --> 00:21:33,560
All they know is they don't
get tractors to sell,

605
00:21:33,593 --> 00:21:34,627
they're out of business.

606
00:21:34,662 --> 00:21:36,262
If an enemy nation
conquered America,

607
00:21:36,295 --> 00:21:37,463
they'd be out of business
for good.

608
00:21:37,497 --> 00:21:38,432
Fat chance.

609
00:21:38,465 --> 00:21:39,700
SERVO: Now go on,
punch me hard.

610
00:21:39,733 --> 00:21:41,602
I'm sorry, major, I'd like
to accommodate you,

611
00:21:41,635 --> 00:21:43,370
but I've spent 10 years
in building up

612
00:21:43,403 --> 00:21:44,672
my sales organization,

613
00:21:44,705 --> 00:21:46,238
and I'm not going
to throw it away

614
00:21:46,272 --> 00:21:47,440
to do the government a favor.

615
00:21:47,473 --> 00:21:50,077
Mr. Sylvester, if people
like you won't cooperate

616
00:21:50,110 --> 00:21:51,645
with your government willingly,

617
00:21:51,678 --> 00:21:54,047
the day may come when we'll be
forced to take over your plants

618
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:55,215
without asking
your permission.

619
00:21:55,248 --> 00:21:56,783
-SERVO: Believe that.
-Over my dead body.

620
00:21:56,817 --> 00:21:58,052
Now, let me tell you something,
major.

621
00:21:58,085 --> 00:21:59,652
-SERVO: Uh-oh. No.
-The people in this country

622
00:21:59,687 --> 00:22:01,220
-will never stand...
-SERVO: Stop it. No.

623
00:22:01,253 --> 00:22:03,490
...so you can go back to
Washington and tell them that.

624
00:22:03,523 --> 00:22:04,892
SERVO: I wanna go.
Leave me alone.

625
00:22:04,925 --> 00:22:06,793
Yes, sir. Just because he was
wearing a major's uniform,

626
00:22:06,827 --> 00:22:08,062
he thought
he could threaten me.

627
00:22:08,095 --> 00:22:10,730
-But I sure told him...
-SERVO: Tom Waits, hey.

628
00:22:10,763 --> 00:22:12,865
Well, your girlfriend
and I got to go.

629
00:22:12,898 --> 00:22:14,567
-MIKE: What?
-Do you believe in drafting

630
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:16,002
women as well as men?

631
00:22:16,035 --> 00:22:19,372
Well, it all depends
on what you wanna draft us for.

632
00:22:19,405 --> 00:22:21,442
CROW:
Whoa, got a live one here.

633
00:22:21,475 --> 00:22:23,877
I mean, labor in a munitions
factory, for instance.

634
00:22:23,910 --> 00:22:26,413
I worked in a factory
for a while during the last war.

635
00:22:26,447 --> 00:22:28,248
-SERVO: Naked.
-They'd ruined my hands,

636
00:22:28,282 --> 00:22:29,482
so I quit.

637
00:22:30,350 --> 00:22:32,385
Another drink, Tim,
for everybody.

638
00:22:32,418 --> 00:22:34,253
SERVO: Oh, Foster Brooks
joins in.

639
00:22:34,287 --> 00:22:36,657
...young lady,
Haraway's my name.

640
00:22:36,690 --> 00:22:38,092
Congressman Haraway.

641
00:22:38,125 --> 00:22:40,060
I'm just in New York
for over the weekend.

642
00:22:40,093 --> 00:22:41,227
It's a pleasure to meet you.

643
00:22:41,260 --> 00:22:42,495
Well, it's nice to know you,
too.

644
00:22:42,528 --> 00:22:43,630
-MIKE: Whatever.
-Valuable work

645
00:22:43,663 --> 00:22:44,732
you're doing, young man,

646
00:22:44,765 --> 00:22:46,767
seeking the voice
of the people.

647
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:49,002
I believe it, and
that's the American way,

648
00:22:49,035 --> 00:22:51,638
-the democratic way.
-Thank you, sir. That's...

649
00:22:51,672 --> 00:22:52,972
MIKE:
This is starting to bang, huh?

650
00:22:53,005 --> 00:22:56,342
CROW: Will somebody
please invade something?

651
00:22:56,375 --> 00:22:59,378
You won't mention the locale
of our interview, of course.

652
00:22:59,412 --> 00:23:02,682
[ Doors closing ]

653
00:23:12,392 --> 00:23:13,993
SERVO: Michael, I must say

654
00:23:14,027 --> 00:23:15,328
that you have prepared
these victuals

655
00:23:15,395 --> 00:23:17,197
with much regalement.

656
00:23:17,230 --> 00:23:18,498
-Indeed.
-Well, thank you, Thomas,

657
00:23:18,532 --> 00:23:21,835
and while we partake
of these aromatic comestibles,

658
00:23:21,868 --> 00:23:23,937
there's no reason
why we cannot converse

659
00:23:23,970 --> 00:23:26,338
in a refined
and dignified manner.

660
00:23:26,372 --> 00:23:28,342
-Ah, yes.
-Indeed.

661
00:23:28,375 --> 00:23:31,478
Say, how about
that sporting event, hmm?

662
00:23:31,512 --> 00:23:33,213
One team emerged the victor,

663
00:23:33,247 --> 00:23:35,348
whereas the other, alas,

664
00:23:35,382 --> 00:23:37,517
found themselves vanquished.

665
00:23:37,550 --> 00:23:40,120
Ah, but such are the
vicissitudes of sport.

666
00:23:40,153 --> 00:23:42,022
Hmm. Indeed.
You know,

667
00:23:42,055 --> 00:23:43,757
the other day,
I had the occasion

668
00:23:43,790 --> 00:23:45,625
to witness a cultural event.

669
00:23:45,658 --> 00:23:47,427
It seemed to point
to the foibles

670
00:23:47,460 --> 00:23:48,795
of everyday living.

671
00:23:48,828 --> 00:23:51,932
Afterwards, I reflected
upon the stimulating nature

672
00:23:51,965 --> 00:23:54,267
-of the presentation.
-Mm-hmm.

673
00:23:54,300 --> 00:23:57,103
Recently, I was taken
by a particular article

674
00:23:57,137 --> 00:23:58,572
of literature.

675
00:23:58,605 --> 00:24:00,840
The author used sentences
and paragraphs

676
00:24:00,873 --> 00:24:02,942
to express his point of view.

677
00:24:02,975 --> 00:24:03,843
Yes. Yes.

678
00:24:03,877 --> 00:24:05,812
Oh, that reminds me
of a pleasantry

679
00:24:05,845 --> 00:24:07,780
I was exposed to recently.

680
00:24:07,813 --> 00:24:09,615
An anecdote happened

681
00:24:09,648 --> 00:24:11,952
that might be interpreted
as whimsical.

682
00:24:11,985 --> 00:24:14,120
It provided all parties involved

683
00:24:14,153 --> 00:24:16,523
with a puckish sense
of diverting amusement.

684
00:24:16,557 --> 00:24:20,260
[ Laughter ]

685
00:24:20,293 --> 00:24:22,762
Mike, this sucks.
Can we just eat?

686
00:24:22,795 --> 00:24:24,163
-Oh, yeah.
-That's a great--

687
00:24:24,197 --> 00:24:25,365
...they're all dead.

688
00:24:25,398 --> 00:24:26,432
I'm making...

689
00:24:26,465 --> 00:24:29,435
[ Indistinct chatter ]

690
00:24:29,468 --> 00:24:32,672
[ Music playing ]

691
00:24:36,475 --> 00:24:38,845
Maybe I'd better get you in...

692
00:24:38,878 --> 00:24:41,180
May I have your name, sir?

693
00:24:41,213 --> 00:24:42,082
My name is Ohman.

694
00:24:42,115 --> 00:24:43,217
-Ohman?
-SERVO: Yes. But my friends

695
00:24:43,250 --> 00:24:45,085
-call me Shotsie.
-...like a band leader,

696
00:24:45,118 --> 00:24:46,152
O-H-M-A-N, isn't it?

697
00:24:46,185 --> 00:24:48,488
SERVO:
Yeah, I get that all the time.

698
00:24:48,522 --> 00:24:49,722
And your occupation, Mr. Ohman?

699
00:24:49,755 --> 00:24:52,192
-SERVO: Communist, I mean...
-I'm a forecaster.

700
00:24:52,225 --> 00:24:53,227
Weather forecaster.

701
00:24:54,327 --> 00:24:56,495
Now, what do you think
of the draft?

702
00:24:56,530 --> 00:24:59,532
I think America wants
new leadership.

703
00:24:59,565 --> 00:25:01,100
-New leadership?
-SERVO: Ja.

704
00:25:01,133 --> 00:25:02,668
What kind of leadership
do you suggest?

705
00:25:02,702 --> 00:25:05,538
-CROW: Puppies.
-I suggest a wizard.

706
00:25:05,572 --> 00:25:07,740
-A what?
-A wizard like Merlin,

707
00:25:07,773 --> 00:25:10,043
who could kill his enemies
by wishing them dead.

708
00:25:10,077 --> 00:25:10,810
MIKE: Poof.

709
00:25:11,845 --> 00:25:14,147
That's the way we like
to beat communism now

710
00:25:14,180 --> 00:25:15,748
by wishing it dead.

711
00:25:15,782 --> 00:25:17,283
SERVO: He's dreamy.

712
00:25:17,317 --> 00:25:19,818
-He's dreamy.
-OHMAN: ...and lower taxes,

713
00:25:19,852 --> 00:25:22,722
labor wants more consumer
products in a 30-hour week.

714
00:25:22,755 --> 00:25:24,958
A college boy wants
a stronger army.

715
00:25:24,992 --> 00:25:27,727
-SERVO: He does?
-And a deferment for himself.

716
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:29,428
The businessman wants
a bigger air force

717
00:25:29,462 --> 00:25:30,663
and a new Cadillac.

718
00:25:30,697 --> 00:25:33,300
-SERVO: He does?
-The housewife wants security

719
00:25:33,333 --> 00:25:35,035
and an electric dishwasher.

720
00:25:35,068 --> 00:25:37,103
CROW:
I just want a working nose.

721
00:25:37,137 --> 00:25:39,072
Everybody wants
a strong America,

722
00:25:39,105 --> 00:25:41,373
and we all want
the same man to pay for it.

723
00:25:41,407 --> 00:25:43,277
SERVO: Ross Perot.

724
00:25:43,310 --> 00:25:45,312
George.

725
00:25:45,345 --> 00:25:46,512
Let George do it.

726
00:25:46,545 --> 00:25:48,482
Ah, ah, I disagree with you.

727
00:25:48,515 --> 00:25:50,383
I don't wanna let George do it.

728
00:25:51,383 --> 00:25:52,785
You must be the exception?

729
00:25:52,818 --> 00:25:54,420
No. I'm George.

730
00:25:54,453 --> 00:25:55,522
[ Laughter ]

731
00:25:55,555 --> 00:25:57,623
SERVO: I like you.
I'll kill you last.

732
00:25:57,657 --> 00:25:59,758
Everyone wants George to do it.

733
00:25:59,792 --> 00:26:01,027
Except George.

734
00:26:01,060 --> 00:26:02,728
SERVO:
I think I just said that joke.

735
00:26:02,762 --> 00:26:04,197
A very good joke,

736
00:26:04,230 --> 00:26:06,767
but wars are not won
by making jokes.

737
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:09,002
SERVO: Ah, every party
needs a pooper.

738
00:26:09,035 --> 00:26:12,038
-To win a war...
-SERVO: You need brandy.

739
00:26:12,072 --> 00:26:15,108
-Lots of it.
-...a nation must concentrate.

740
00:26:16,408 --> 00:26:19,678
SERVO: I could use a man like
you in my organization. Norm!

741
00:26:19,712 --> 00:26:20,713
-CROW: Norm!
-SERVO: Norm!

742
00:26:20,747 --> 00:26:22,915
MIKE: Must go home
and kill parents.

743
00:26:23,950 --> 00:26:25,685
SERVO: Plenty of scotch.

744
00:26:25,718 --> 00:26:27,220
Very nice legs, yes.

745
00:26:27,253 --> 00:26:30,257
[ Music playing ]

746
00:26:37,063 --> 00:26:39,665
CROW: Oh, that reminds me,
I have to clean the toilet bowl.

747
00:26:39,698 --> 00:26:41,768
[ Music playing ]

748
00:26:41,802 --> 00:26:42,902
MIKE: What's that say?

749
00:26:42,935 --> 00:26:45,238
Mastery not victory?

750
00:26:47,440 --> 00:26:48,808
CROW: Castro's an okay guy.

751
00:26:48,842 --> 00:26:50,810
CROW: Dalton Trumbo's
a good screenwriter.

752
00:26:50,843 --> 00:26:52,912
CROW: Roy Cohn
is our greatest obstacle,

753
00:26:52,945 --> 00:26:54,780
I wonder if there are
any beer nuts left.

754
00:26:54,813 --> 00:26:58,150
[ Music playing ]

755
00:26:58,183 --> 00:26:59,752
MIKE: Mentiary.

756
00:27:00,720 --> 00:27:02,522
SERVO: Hey turn it up,
Max Headroom's on.

757
00:27:02,555 --> 00:27:04,123
CROW: Excuse me,
I have to go

758
00:27:04,157 --> 00:27:06,258
to the little ubermensch's room.

759
00:27:06,292 --> 00:27:08,260
